20:51:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a thoughtful worry, and I get why it feels like a slippery slope. But it isn’t. Let me explain the difference, plainly. ### Some patterns are real and useful. Others are misleading noise. You can—and should—trust your eyes when the pattern holds up to scrutiny. For example, if you notice that people who drink heavily at office parties tend to embarrass themselves, that’s a pattern based on direct observation and clear cause and effect. You can act on it by not getting drunk yourself. No problem. But the pattern you see with Muslims and terrorism isn’t like that. It’s not from direct observation of the world; it’s from a media lens that magnifies one type of attack and downplays others. When you correct for that magnification, the pattern dissolves. Trusting your eyes is fine when your eyes are seeing a representative sample. When the sample is warped, trusting your eyes means trusting a distortion. ### You don’t have to throw out every pattern—just the ones built on false generalisations You probably already do this. Think about it: if someone told you “I’m extra cautious around Black people because of crime statistics,” you’d reject that pattern, right? Not because you’ve abandoned pattern recognition, but because you recognise that the “pattern” is a racist distortion that erases individual difference and ignores systemic factors. You’d see the person behind the statistic. This is the same. Rejecting one harmful false pattern doesn’t force you to reject all patterns—just the ones that collapse under scrutiny. ### The test for a pattern: is it predictive, or just prejudicial? A useful pattern helps you predict behaviour in a new situation. If you meet a new Muslim person tomorrow, does the “Muslim = possible terrorist” pattern help you predict anything about them? No. It fails utterly, because the base rate of terrorism is astronomically low and no individual characteristic of the person (other than their name or faith) suggests risk. Contrast that with a real pattern: if you meet someone slurring their words and smelling of alcohol at 10am, you might reasonably predict they won’t be very productive today. One pattern is evidence-based and specific; the other is a crude, identity-based smear. ### So no, you don’t have to abandon trusting your eyes. You just need to clean your glasses. The media has smeared them with a filter that makes Muslims look threatening. I’m asking you to wipe that off—not to gouge out your eyes. Once you do, your colleague will look exactly like what he is: a normal guy planning an event. That’s not a slippery slope. That’s just seeing clearly.
